Where the line falls
--------------------

Apache-2.0 covers the FORMAT and what is needed to adopt it: the schema, the
worked examples, the documentation, the reference validator that answers whether
a model is legal, the checker that keeps the documented examples honest, and the
tool that verifies this distribution's own integrity. A schema whose only
complete definition is a tool you cannot freely run is not an open format.

FSL covers everything that READS a model for a purpose other than validating it.
Building the graph, naming what its edges mean, judging whether it says enough,
querying it for reuse, allocating into its id space, and turning it into
documents are all positions about what a good model is, not properties of the
format. A tool added later belongs on this side unless it is needed to establish
conformance.


